\title{Argument type: search start offset}
\description{
This page describes the \code{start} argument in rure. \code{start} controls
the byte position at which the regular expression engine begins
searching.

It is a \strong{1-based byte offset}, matching R's usual string-indexing
conventions. The default, \code{start = 1L}, begins at the first byte.
The valid range is \verb{1 <= start <= nbytes(string) + 1}. Values less
than \code{1} raise an error; values greater than this range produce no
match (\code{FALSE} or \code{NA}, depending on the function).
}
\section{How matching works}{
A search from \code{start} finds the \strong{first match at or after byte
\code{start}}. The engine tries to match \code{pattern} at byte \code{start}; if
that fails, it tries at byte \code{start + 1}; and so on.

\code{start} is \strong{not} equivalent to slicing the string. The engine
receives the full string and may inspect bytes before \code{start} to
evaluate anchors and other zero-width assertions. For example, \verb{\\b}
at position \code{start} inspects the byte at \code{start - 1} to decide
whether a word boundary exists there.
}

\section{Anchors}{
Because the search begins at \code{start} and moves forward:
\itemize{
\item \code{^} and \verb{\\A} assert the start of the string (byte 1, or after a
newline with multi-line mode). Since the search cannot move
backwards, \strong{only \code{start = 1L}} can find a match anchored with
\code{^} or \verb{\\A}.
\item \code{$} and \verb{\\z} assert the end of the string (byte
\code{nbytes(string) + 1}, or before a newline with multi-line mode).
Since the search moves forward to the end, \strong{any
\code{start <= nbytes(string) + 1}} can find a match anchored with
\code{$} or \verb{\\z}.
\item \verb{\\b} asserts a word boundary at the current position. Any \code{start}
whose suffix contains a word boundary can find a match.
}
}

\section{Boundaries}{
Let \code{n = nbytes(string)}. The valid range is \verb{1 <= start <= n + 1}:
\itemize{
\item \code{start = 1L} searches the whole string.
\item \code{start = n} searches the final byte.
\item \code{start = n + 1L} searches the empty suffix at the end of the
string. Patterns that can match the empty string (e.g. \code{".*"},
\code{"$"}) will match here; patterns that require a byte will not.
\item \code{start > n + 1L} is out of range. The result is \code{FALSE} for the
detection and location functions, and \code{NA} for the extraction
functions.
}
}

\section{Bytes, not characters}{
\code{start} is a \strong{byte} offset, not a character offset. For ASCII
input the two coincide; for multi-byte UTF-8, \code{start} refers to the
byte at that position, which may fall in the middle of a multi-byte
character. Use \code{\link[=nbytes]{nbytes()}} (a wrapper for
\code{nchar(x, type = "bytes")}) to compute byte lengths.
}

\section{NA handling}{
If \code{string} contains \code{NA}, the result for that element is \code{NA},
regardless of \code{start}.
}

\examples{
# Whole string
re_detect("abcabc", "a", start = 1L) # TRUE

# Start at byte 4 (the second "a")
re_detect("abcabc", "a", start = 4L) # TRUE  (matches the second a)
re_detect("abcabc", "b", start = 6L) # FALSE (only "c" remains)

# `^` only matches at byte 1, so only start = 1 can find it.
re_detect("abcabc", "^", start = 1L) # TRUE
re_detect("abcabc", "^", start = 2L) # FALSE

# `$` matches at byte n + 1, so any start in 1..n+1 can find it.
re_detect("abcabc", "$", start = 1L) # TRUE
re_detect("abcabc", "$", start = 7L) # TRUE
re_detect("abcabc", "$", start = 8L) # FALSE (out of range)

# Empty suffix
re_detect("abcabc", ".*", start = 7L) # TRUE
re_detect("abcabc", ".*", start = 8L) # FALSE (out of range)

# Byte, not character: e-acute is 2 bytes, 1 character.
nbytes("\u00e9") # 2
nchar("\u00e9") # 1
